/* Pass 23A: canonical chapter parity for both Avatar narratives. */
(function(){'use strict';const lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;const changes={"jasmine-after-rain":{"avatar-09":{"number":9,"title":"Tea for Two","content":"Tea for two became a habit before either of us admitted it was a ritual.\n\nEvery evening after closing, Iroh poured one cup for me and one for himself. Some nights I sat in silence. Some nights I played pai sho. Once, I spent an entire hour arguing about whether a particular pastry was improved or ruined by sesame seeds.\n\n“It is improved,” Iroh said.\n\n“It is overwhelmed.”\n\n“The sesame is subtle.”\n\n“The sesame is shouting.”\n\n“You have not met enough sesame.”\n\nHe had begun saving the corner table for me. I teased him about it. He denied it while moving the sign that said RESERVED whenever I arrived.\n\nThe routine gave the days a gentle shape. Work. Tea. Conversation. Sleep above the shop while Iroh's footsteps moved below.\n\nI had stopped feeling like a guest.\n\nThen a woman from the market began visiting every afternoon.\n\nShe was a merchant with bright scarves and a laugh that filled the shop. She knew Iroh from years before, when he had traveled through the city. She called him “General” in a teasing tone and touched his arm when she spoke.\n\nThe first time, I thought nothing of it.\n\nThe third time, I broke a cup.\n\nThe sound silenced the room.\n\n“I’m sorry,” I said.\n\nIroh looked toward me. The merchant looked too.\n\n“No harm done,” Iroh said. “The cup had a dramatic life.”\n\nI cleaned the pieces. My fingers shook.\n\nThe merchant watched me. “You are new.”\n\n“Not very.”\n\n“I have not seen you before.”\n\n“You were not looking.”\n\nIroh's eyebrows rose.\n\nThe merchant laughed. “I like this one.”\n\n“I am not a tea blend,” I said.\n\n“Everything is a blend.”\n\nShe looked at Iroh. “You have become selective.”\n\n“I have become tired,” he replied.\n\nThe merchant ordered a pot to take home and left. The door closed behind her.\n\nI carried the broken cup to the kitchen.\n\nIroh followed.\n\n“Are you angry?” he asked.\n\n“No.”\n\n“You are washing a cup that no longer exists.”\n\nI looked down. Water ran over my hands.\n\n“I’m embarrassed.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because I was jealous.”\n\nIroh turned off the water. “Jealousy is not a crime.”\n\n“It felt ugly.”\n\n“Fear often does.”\n\n“You know her.”\n\n“I do.”\n\n“She knows the version of you from before the tea shop.”\n\n“She knows a version.”\n\n“She called you General.”\n\n“Many people have.”\n\n“She touched you.”\n\nIroh's expression remained calm, but his eyes became very gentle. “Would you like to touch me?”\n\nThe question made me look at him.\n\n“That is not fair.”\n\n“It is not a trick.”\n\n“You cannot solve jealousy by offering me your arm.”\n\n“No. But I can remind you that her familiarity does not create a claim on me.”\n\nI folded the wet cloth. “Do you want her?”\n\n“No.”\n\nThe answer was immediate.\n\n“Did you ever?”\n\n“Once. A very long time ago.”\n\n“And now?”\n\n“Now I look forward to one person's footsteps.”\n\nMy throat tightened.\n\n“You are going to say it is me.”\n\n“It is you.”\n\n“You could be more subtle.”\n\n“I have been subtle. You broke a cup.”\n\nI looked away. “I don’t want you to stop seeing her because of me.”\n\n“I will not.”\n\nThe directness steadied me.\n\n“But I want to know where I stand,” I said.\n\n“You stand beside me.”\n\n“That’s a nice phrase.”\n\n“It is also a location.”\n\n“You’re impossible.”\n\n“So I have been told.”\n\nIroh took my hands and dried them with a clean towel. His fingers moved slowly over my knuckles.\n\n“I am not asking you to be without fear,” he said. “I am asking you to bring it to me before it becomes a story you believe without evidence.”\n\n“What evidence do I have?”\n\n“You have my time. My attention. My honesty. You have the room above my shop and a key to the back door.”\n\nI looked at him. “You gave me a key?”\n\n“You have been using it for two weeks.”\n\n“I thought it was the kitchen key.”\n\n“It opens the back door as well.”\n\n“You could have told me.”\n\n“I enjoy watching you discover things.”\n\n“That is a terrible habit.”\n\n“I am old. I have earned a few.”\n\nI laughed despite myself.\n\nHe lifted my hand and kissed it. “You are not a replacement for anyone.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You are not competing with my past.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You are the person I am choosing now.”\n\nThe words made my eyes sting.\n\n“Can I kiss you?” he asked.\n\nI stepped closer. “You can.”\n\nThe kiss began in the kitchen, between shelves of tea and a bucket of broken pottery. Iroh's hand settled at my waist. I held the front of his vest, feeling the warmth beneath it.\n\nThe kiss was warm and unhurried. Iroh paused to check that I still wanted it, and I smiled before answering. We stayed close until the kettle cooled, then returned to our tea.\n\n“Still comfortable?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Do you wish to stop?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Then we may continue.”\n\nThe tea shop was dark beyond the kitchen. The kettle had cooled. The city moved outside without knowing that my world had become a pair of hands, a careful question, and an answer I was allowed to change.\n\nWhen Iroh finally drew back, his forehead rested against mine.\n\n“The cup can be replaced,” he said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“The important things are not so easily broken.”\n\n“You are talking about us.”\n\n“I am talking about trust.”\n\n“That is less romantic.”\n\n“It is more durable.”\n\nI kissed him again.\n\nThe next morning, a new cup sat at my place at the corner table. It was painted with a green dragon and a small white lotus.\n\nOn the bottom, Iroh had written: FOR TEA, NOT FOR THROWING.\n\nI kept it.\n\nThe cup became part of the shop's daily rhythm. Iroh placed it at my table before I arrived. I threatened to use it as evidence in every future argument. He claimed the painting made it impossible to throw.\n\n“You could have made it ugly,” I said.\n\n“I considered painting a kettle on it.”\n\n“That would have been more accurate.”\n\n“The dragon was more dignified.”\n\n“You are the dragon.”\n\n“I am a man who owns a tea shop.”\n\n“You can be both.”\n\nHe studied me over the rim of his cup. “You have been listening.”\n\n“Occasionally.”\n\n“It is a good habit.”\n\nThe merchant with the bright scarves did not return for several days.\n\nIn the meantime, a potter arrived to collect the shards of my broken cup. I had expected him to quote a replacement price and leave. Instead, he held two pieces together against the light and frowned.\n\n\"That wasn't the new glaze,\" he said. \"See the hairline? I missed it when I fired the rim.\"\n\n\"I was holding it when it broke.\"\n\n\"You were. Doesn't make the flaw your fault.\" He named the kiln batch, then asked Mina whether any of the other cups had cracked. She fetched three from the shelf. Two were sound; the third had a pale line beneath its handle.\n\nIroh watched the exchange without interrupting. In another life, he had commanded people who would have been afraid to tell him that a shipment was defective. The potter wasn't afraid. He was annoyed about the wasted clay and determined that nobody burn their fingers.\n\n\"We'll take those off the service shelf,\" Mina said.\n\n\"And I will replace them,\" the potter replied. \"Give me a week.\"\n\nAfter he left, I found Iroh examining the unbroken cup I'd been using. He set it down when he noticed me looking.\n\n\"You're worried I'll break another.\"\n\n\"I am worried that I failed to see the crack before you cut yourself.\"\n\n\"Those aren't the same worry.\"\n\n\"No,\" he said. \"They are not.\"\n\nThe merchant's easy touch had unsettled me; Iroh had known it. We still had a conversation to finish about his past and what I needed to know. But the cups were the potter's problem to repair, and my feelings were mine to name. He didn't pour tea over either problem and pretend they had vanished. When she did, she brought Iroh a bundle of dried flowers.\n\n“For the shop,” she said.\n\n“Thank you,” he replied.\n\nShe looked at me. “You are still here.”\n\n“I am.”\n\n“Good.”\n\nI waited until she left before speaking.\n\n“Good?”\n\nIroh arranged the flowers in a jar. “She was pleased.”\n\n“That sounded like approval.”\n\n“It was.”\n\n“Does everyone in your life have opinions about us?”\n\n“Everyone has opinions about everything.”\n\n“That is not comforting.”\n\n“It is freeing. Their opinions are abundant, but they are not commands.”\n\nI looked at the cup with my name. “You are good at saying things I need to hear.”\n\n“I try to say what is true.”\n\n“How do I know?”\n\nIroh stepped closer. “You do not have to know all at once. Watch what I do when the truth is inconvenient.”\n\nThe answer was not a demand for trust. It was permission to let trust grow slowly.\n\nI touched his sleeve. “I can do that.”\n\nHe turned his hand and caught my fingers.\n\n“And I can watch what you do,” he said.\n\n“What do I do?”\n\n“You return. Even when you are embarrassed. Even when you are angry. Even when you believe you have made a mistake.”\n\n“I return because you have good tea.”\n\n“Of course.”\n\n“And pastries.”\n\n“Naturally.”\n\nHis smile was warm. The teasing was becoming a language of its own.\n\nBefore closing, a former Fire Nation officer entered and called Iroh “General” in a voice that made everyone at the corner table look up.\n\n“Former general,” Iroh corrected. “Today I sell tea.”\n\nThe visitor wanted a private meeting. Iroh gave him the public menu. It was a remarkably polite way of refusing a summons, and I recognized the calculation behind it: he had placed the man where the whole room could see him, denied him an audience, and made it impossible to complain without embarrassing himself.\n\nAfter the man left, I said, “You still arrange people like pieces on a board.”\n\nIroh did not smile. “Sometimes I do.”\n\n“Mina told you yesterday that her afternoon belonged to her.”\n\nHis fingers paused over the abandoned teacup. “I have commanded thousands. It is considerably more difficult to learn when not to command one person.”\n\nHe took out the staff rota, found the extra minutes Mina had worked, and added the wages he owed her. It was not the kind of apology that would impress an audience. It was the sort that cost something.\n\n“Are you going to ask me to remind you?” I said.\n\n“No. You will remind me whether I ask or not.”\n\n“Very perceptive.”\n\n“Deeply inconvenient.”\n\nWe laughed, but I held his gaze. His past mattered. So did the person standing across from me now, willing to be corrected without turning my concern into another lesson.\n\nWhen his fingers brushed mine on the counter, he waited instead of closing his hand. I turned my palm over. He took the invitation, and the small silence that followed felt more intimate than a speech.\n\n\nThat evening, I washed the new cup by hand. Iroh stood beside me, drying it with a soft cloth.\n\n“You do not have to keep it at the shop,” he said.\n\n“I want to.”\n\n“Why?”\n\nI looked at the dragon and lotus painted around the rim. “Because it reminds me that I can be wanted without being swallowed.”\n\nIroh's expression changed. He set down the cloth.\n\n“Come here,” he said.\n\n“That sounded like an order.”\n\n“Then consider it an invitation.”\n\nI stepped into his arms.\n\nThe kiss was gentle. It lasted long enough for the kettle to cool and the last lantern outside to dim.\n\nWhen I parted, Iroh rested his forehead against mine.\n\n“You are not a cup,” he said.\n\n“I am aware.”\n\n“You are not something I can place safely on a shelf.”\n\n“That would be uncomfortable.”\n\n“You are a person who can leave.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“And return.”\n\nI smiled. “I know that too.”\n\nHe kissed me once more before the shop's bell announced a late customer.\n\nWe both stepped apart, laughing softly, and returned to work.\n\nThe new cup waited between us.\n\nIts painted dragon faced the door. Its lotus faced me. I decided that was a good arrangement.\n\nIroh agreed.\n\nThe cup stayed safe.\n\nSo did the trust between us.","word_count":2050},"avatar-24":{"number":24,"title":"A Question Worth Asking","content":"I asked Iroh who he was when nobody wanted anything from him.\n\nThe question came out while we were carrying the last dry towels to the balcony above the Jasmine Dragon. Below us, Ba Sing Se filled with evening carts and the smell of cooking oil. Someone had started singing three streets away, loudly and not particularly well.\n\nIroh looked up at the sky. \"A man who likes a quiet balcony.\"\n\n\"That's a place, not a person.\"\n\n\"A man who has brought you to a very good place.\"\n\n\"You're doing it again.\"\n\nHe took the towels from me and made a show of folding them. \"What am I doing?\"\n\n\"Answering the question that makes you sound most helpful.\"\n\nHe stopped folding.\n\nI wished, for one terrible moment, that I'd simply asked whether he wanted more tea. It would have been easier. But I wasn't falling in love with an answer to every problem. I was falling for someone who could make a joke, and make another joke, and still carry something so heavy I sometimes saw it in the way he stood up.\n\nIroh sat on the little bench. There was room beside him, but he didn't pat it. He waited.\n\n\"I used to be many things,\" he said. \"Some of them I did not choose. Others I chose badly.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I am also a man who sings off-key when he thinks no one can hear.\"\n\n\"That one's new.\"\n\n\"It is a carefully guarded secret.\"\n\nFrom the street, the unknown singer missed another note so spectacularly Iroh winced. Then he began humming the same tune and made it worse on purpose. I laughed hard enough to lean against the railing.\n\n\"You're terrible.\"\n\n\"Then I have answered at least one part of your question.\"\n\nWe sat for a while. The breeze lifted the edge of his sleeve. I noticed a tiny scorch mark from the kettle he had nearly dropped that afternoon while arguing with a supplier over the cost of dried jasmine. I touched the fabric.\n\n\"Does this happen often?\"\n\n\"Only when I am attempting to look dignified.\"\n\n\"So daily.\"\n\n\"An outrageous accusation.\"\n\nI pulled my chair closer. \"I mean it. What would you do if nobody was waiting for advice, nobody called you General or Uncle, and you could choose the afternoon for yourself?\"\n\nHe thought longer than I expected.\n\n\"I would eat something sweet that I had no intention of sharing,\" he admitted. \"I would play Pai Sho badly, so no one invited me to a serious match. I would visit a garden where nobody knew my name.\"\n\n\"Could you do any of that tomorrow?\"\n\n\"Perhaps not the garden. Mina needs me to sign the delivery order, and Lian is waiting for us to repair the hose.\"\n\n\"That's two things. Not an entire lifetime.\"\n\nHe smiled, but I could see the uncertainty beneath it.\n\n\"I've spent so much of my life being useful in order to justify the room I occupied,\" he said. \"I find it difficult to imagine occupying a room for pleasure.\"\n\nI reached for his hand, then stopped. \"Would you like me to hold it?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nWe stayed on the balcony while a delivery cart rattled through the street below. A boy carrying buns stopped to argue with the driver about whose wheel had splashed water onto his shoes. Iroh leaned over the railing as though he might settle the matter, then drew back.\n\n\"That was very difficult,\" he admitted.\n\n\"What was?\"\n\n\"Allowing people to quarrel without offering a solution.\"\n\nI laughed. \"You can care about them without being appointed referee.\"\n\nHe sat beside me. His sleeve brushed mine, warm from the tea kettle we'd carried upstairs.\n\n\"Would you like to know something else I want?\" he asked.\n\n\"Very much.\"\n\n\"I want to be allowed to be wrong about a little thing, and not have it become evidence that I am unworthy of the life I have now.\"\n\nIt was such a quiet wish that it frightened me. I knew how many enormous mistakes stood behind his words, and how little right I had to offer absolution for harm that wasn't mine.\n\n\"I can argue with you about a burnt bun,\" I said. \"I won't use it to make a verdict on every part of your history.\"\n\nHe looked relieved, then immediately suspicious.\n\n\"You would argue about a bun?\"\n\n\"Passionately.\"\n\n\"Then you may have found your calling.\"\n\nHe stood and fetched two sweet buns from the basket we'd brought upstairs, placing them between us as though preparing for an important contest. One was slightly darker along the edge.\n\n\"Which is better?\"\n\nI took a bite of each. \"Neither. They're different.\"\n\n\"An evasion.\"\n\n\"You're the one who spent half the evening evading my questions.\"\n\nHe laughed and conceded the point. Then he asked what I wanted when nobody needed me. The question caught me by surprise.\n\n\"I want to write,\" I said. \"I want to see people I love without having to explain why loving them doesn't diminish anyone else. And sometimes I want to sit beside you without fixing anything.\"\n\nHe took my hand again. \"Then let us practice the last one.\"\n\nWe sat until the evening grew cool, sharing the buns and saying nothing important. That too was a kind of answer.\n\nHis palm was warm. It still surprised me when something that simple felt like a choice two people had made together rather than an answer one person owed.\n\n\"I'm not asking you to stop helping people,\" I said. \"But you can want things that have nothing to do with rescuing me.\"\n\n\"And what do you want?\"\n\n\"I want you to stop making me guess whether there's a person behind the wise expression.\"\n\n\"Very demanding.\"\n\n\"Extremely.\"\n\nHe turned toward me. \"Then I will tell you something selfish. When you leave to spend time with people you also love, I sometimes look at the upper room and wish you were in it. It is not a wish I intend to use against you.\"\n\nI swallowed. We'd spoken plainly about my ethical non-monogamy and my preference for polyamory. Iroh had not been asked to pretend that he never missed me. I just didn't want longing to become a debt.\n\n\"You can say you miss me,\" I told him. \"You can ask whether I'm free next week. I want to hear what you need, not discover it after you've become lonely enough to be noble about it.\"\n\nHis laugh was smaller this time. \"Noble loneliness is a remarkably foolish habit.\"\n\n\"One you've perfected.\"\n\n\"I am reconsidering my achievements.\"\n\nIroh found a folded paper in the pocket of his robe and turned it over twice before giving it to me. I thought it was a letter until I saw the little squares drawn beside each name.\n\n“A rota?” I asked.\n\n“A habit,” he said. “Old men are allowed to have them.”\n\nThe names belonged to people from the White Lotus who had written offering to help at the shop. Beside each name he had marked a day, a task, and an hour. He had not asked any of them whether the hours suited.\n\n“You're recruiting an army to wash cups.”\n\n“A very small army.”\n\n“An army of people with lives.”\n\nHe looked at the squares again. “Ah.”\n\nHe took the paper downstairs before I could suggest a better system. Mina was closing the cash drawer and didn't look thrilled to see another schedule.\n\n\"These are volunteers?\" she asked.\n\n\"Old friends who offered assistance.\"\n\n\"Did they offer to wash cups at dawn?\"\n\n\"No,\" Iroh said, after a pause. \"They did not.\"\n\nMina turned the sheet over. On the back, in a hand much less elegant than his, she drew three columns: work we actually needed, times the shop could accommodate it, and people who had agreed to come. Iroh looked at the empty third column as though it were an unfamiliar variation of Pai Sho.\n\n\"Where do I put my friends?\" he asked.\n\n\"Where they put themselves,\" she said.\n\nA courier came by the following morning with two replies. One friend could help deliver leaves after the midday market but had no interest in serving customers. Another wasn't coming at all; his granddaughter was visiting, and he meant to spend the weekend with her.\n\nIroh read that answer twice.\n\n\"You're not disappointed?\" I asked.\n\n\"I am,\" he said. \"I would enjoy seeing him. But he has chosen well. I hope his granddaughter is less foolish than the old man who keeps scheduling him.\"\n\nMina heard that from the other room and called out, \"An excellent start.\"\n\nI caught Iroh smiling into his cup. That afternoon he went to the garden simply because it was a garden, not because the plants required his strategic intervention. I followed for the walk, and he didn't use the quiet as an excuse to ask where I'd be every evening of the month.\n\nThe balcony door rattled in the wind. He put his thumb over the top of the page, almost protective. I was beginning to see the distance between how he cared for people and how he tried to keep them safe. A roster could be an expression of love. It could also be an order disguised as generosity.\n\n“Do you want help asking them?” I said.\n\n“No.” Then he saw my expression. “I mean I would like to ask them myself. Without the comforting shield of an officer's stationery.”\n\nHe took out his brush and drew a line through the assigned hours. On the other side of the page he wrote three questions instead: *Would you like to visit? What would you enjoy doing? When would suit you?*\n\n“That is a great deal less efficient,” he said.\n\n“Terribly.”\n\n“And perhaps more likely to result in visitors who want to be here.”\n\nI leaned against him. He was warm from the kitchen, and his sleeve smelled of roasted leaves. I wondered whether a man could spend a lifetime learning the difference between being needed and being wanted. Then I decided I didn't have to answer it for him.\n\n“I'll make you a deal,” I said. “You can be wrong about the roster if I get to be wrong about the next burnt bun.”\n\n“An alarming concession. I shall consider it.”\n\nHe folded the paper again. This time the blank side faced outward.\n\n\nThe shop bell rang downstairs. Mina called that the parcel runner had delivered the repaired tea strainer and wanted a receipt. Iroh rose, then glanced at me.\n\n\"Would you mind if I took care of that?\"\n\n\"Not at all. I'll still be here.\"\n\nHe went down and came back five minutes later with a paper bag.\n\n\"Sweet buns,\" he announced. \"Bought with my own money, so the inventory will not suffer.\"\n\n\"You bought two.\"\n\n\"I have not yet learned selfishness perfectly.\"\n\nWe ate them on the bench. He got sugar on his beard. I considered telling him, then decided I liked the excuse to lean closer. I wiped it away with my thumb. He caught my wrist gently.\n\n\"May I kiss you?\" he asked.\n\n\"Please.\"\n\nWe kissed until the evening voices rose and the last light slipped between the rooftops. When we broke apart, he rested his head against my shoulder for a while.\n\n\"Call me Iroh when we're alone,\" he said.\n\n\"I already do.\"\n\n\"Then keep doing it. I like the way you say it.\"\n\nBelow, the city went on needing things. Above it, the man beside me wanted another sweet bun and someone to laugh with. That was more than enough for tonight.","word_count":1926},"avatar-39":{"number":39,"title":"The Tea We Make","content":"The first cup was too bitter.\n\nThe second tasted like someone had peeled an orange into a campfire.\n\nIroh considered the third with the grave attention of a general reviewing a battlefield.\n\n\"I believe,\" he said, \"we have declared war on tea.\"\n\n\"You're the one who told me to keep the smoke-dried peel.\"\n\n\"I said the idea was interesting.\"\n\n\"Those are not the same words.\"\n\n\"No. I may have chosen them carefully.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nThe kitchen at the Jasmine Dragon was warm from the stove, but rain cooled the window enough for fog to collect along the glass. The morning rush had finished. The staff were eating in the back room, and we had finally claimed an hour to experiment with a blend we could honestly call ours.\n\nNot a famous tea from Iroh's past. Not a magical ingredient from Lanternweald. Something made by two people who liked different flavors and were learning when compromise improved a thing and when it merely diluted it.\n\nOn the table lay three jars, a small mortar, a notebook, a receipt, and one sprig of the not-tea vine I'd brought back from a courtyard visit weeks earlier.\n\nIroh tapped the sprig.\n\n\"That plant still refuses to belong in my teapot.\"\n\n\"Not everything green needs to.\"\n\n\"A revolutionary theory.\"\n\nHe smiled, but then he reached for the old recipe card.\n\nI caught the movement.\n\n\"We said we'd start without a traditional formula.\"\n\n\"I was only checking the steeping time.\"\n\n\"You're checking the cinnamon ratio.\"\n\nHe looked down.\n\n\"Perhaps.\"\n\nI understood why the card comforted him. It had notes written in a hand I didn't recognize, corrections added over years, and a faint stain at the edge. It carried more than instructions. There were people in its margins.\n\n\"I'm not asking you to throw it away,\" I said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I just want you to taste what we made before deciding it needs to become something you've already served.\"\n\nIroh set the card aside.\n\n\"Fair.\"\n\nWe had a practical reason to experiment. The last delivery of fragrant mountain leaves was smaller than expected, and the familiar premium blend would cost too much to serve as the everyday house cup. The staff had asked for a more affordable option that still tasted like something they were proud to pour.\n\nI wanted to solve the problem by adding more citrus.\n\nIroh wanted to solve it with patience.\n\nNeither was a complete method.\n\nMina, who had been covering the morning service, came in carrying a tray of empty cups.\n\n\"Are you making the kitchen smell like a burned orchard on purpose?\" she asked.\n\nIroh bowed his head.\n\n\"We are exploring possibilities.\"\n\n\"Well, the possibilities have escaped under the door.\"\n\nI covered my mouth.\n\nMina examined the jars.\n\n\"How much does each cup cost?\"\n\nIroh paused.\n\n\"That is a very sensible question.\"\n\n\"Someone has to ask it,\" she said.\n\nWe put aside the tasting spoons and worked through the actual ingredients. The dried peel was inexpensive, but preparing it took time. The better base leaves were reliable but had to be ordered before the market day. Honey would make every cup sweeter and considerably more expensive; it would also make the recipe unsuitable for customers who didn't want added sweetener.\n\n\"What if we leave sweetening to the person drinking it?\" I asked.\n\nMina looked at me.\n\n\"Then I don't have to explain why the house blend suddenly costs twice as much.\"\n\n\"Sold.\"\n\n\"Not yet,\" Iroh said. \"We must first make it drinkable.\"\n\nMina laughed and returned to work.\n\nI rewrote the recipe with three columns: flavor, cost, and time. Iroh adjusted the charcoal heat so the kettle warmed more gently. We made two small batches with the same base leaf, adding the smoke-dried peel to one and fresh citrus zest to the other.\n\nHe tasted the smoky version.\n\n\"Still a little fierce.\"\n\n\"That's one word.\"\n\n\"Would you prefer 'angry'?\"\n\n\"Accurate.\"\n\nWe reduced the peel and tested again. The next cup was softer. I could taste the tea before the smoke, with a bright citrus note that arrived only after I'd swallowed.\n\nIroh went quiet.\n\nThat was usually a good sign.\n\n\"Well?\" I asked.\n\nHe sipped again.\n\n\"Unexpected.\"\n\n\"Good unexpected or terrible unexpected?\"\n\nHe studied me above the rim.\n\n\"Good. A little impertinent. Very much like someone I know.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"Does that person have excellent taste?\"\n\n\"On occasion.\"\n\n\"Careful. You're going to start sounding affectionate.\"\n\nHe reached across the table and touched my hand.\n\n\"I was rather hoping to.\"\n\nThe kitchen seemed suddenly warmer.\n\nI thought about the first times we'd talked openly about romance, about my being ethically non-monogamous and preferring polyamory. I hadn't asked him to become my only home or to pretend his own history was smaller than it was. He had asked for honesty and the freedom to say when a plan made him uneasy. We had both kept asking, even when the answers were inconvenient.\n\n\"Can I ask something?\" I said.\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\n\"When you call this *our* tea, does that mean you want me here every day?\"\n\nHis expression changed.\n\n\"No. It means you helped make it, and your contribution belongs on the label. You have other places you love. I would not turn a jar into a leash.\"\n\nRelief hit me hard enough that I laughed.\n\n\"That was a very specific fear.\"\n\n\"Then I am glad you named it.\"\n\nWe wrote the first label together. My handwriting leaned to the right; his was carefully shaped. The two lines looked terrible beside each other.\n\n\"Absolutely dreadful,\" I said.\n\n\"Unique,\" Iroh corrected.\n\nMina returned and asked to taste the final batch before we named it. She took a sip, made a thoughtful noise, and asked how long the peel had been dried.\n\n\"Long enough that I nearly gave up,\" I said.\n\n\"Then the drying time needs to be on the recipe.\"\n\nI added it.\n\nShe checked the price and nodded.\n\nThe afternoon brought an unexpected visitor. Greg came through the Starwake crossing with a guitar case, a jacket that had lost an argument with the weather and no patience for pretending he understood tea.\n\n\"Jasper said you were experimenting,\" he told Iroh. \"I mostly know coffee. Is there, uh, a polite way to taste something and say it needs work?\"\n\nIroh offered him a cup. \"You could say it needs work.\"\n\nGreg took a cautious sip. His eyebrows climbed. \"Oh, hey. That's actually nice. Kind of like somebody lit an orange peel on fire and then apologized.\"\n\nI laughed so hard I had to put down the spoon.\n\n\"That,\" Iroh said, \"is the most unusual tasting note I have received this week.\"\n\n\"Sorry. I can make it more official. Orange, smoke, uh... enthusiastic leaf?\"\n\n\"Please do not,\" Mina called from the counter. \"I'm trying to write a menu people can understand.\"\n\nGreg set the cup down. He asked what the leaves cost, whether the recipe would be consistent next week and if it needed the expensive mountain variety he'd heard me complain about. He sounded less like a connoisseur than a man who had struggled to buy sound cables on a budget.\n\nIroh showed him the smaller-batch figures. He didn't turn it into a lecture about finding one's inner harmony. He discussed the weight of leaves, the drying time and the point at which the smoke overpowered the cup. Greg listened and nodded.\n\n\"Yeah, like turning the amp up until nobody can hear the singer,\" he said.\n\n\"Not precisely,\" Iroh answered. Then his face lit with genuine interest. \"But I think I see the resemblance.\"\n\nThey argued cheerfully for ten minutes about whether practice or equipment mattered more. Greg left with a small sample to try on his next quiet night. Iroh watched the door close.\n\n\"He is an interesting fellow,\" he said.\n\n\"Very.\"\n\n\"And he does not approve of overworked amplifiers.\"\n\n\"Neither should you,\" I said. He looked at the kettle with exaggerated suspicion.\n\n\"I can serve that.\"\n\nComing from Mina, the compliment meant more than a poem.\n\nWe offered a small sample to the afternoon customers. Halen, who had never trusted a new tea simply because Iroh liked it, asked for an ordinary cup first and compared the two.\n\n\"The new one is strange,\" he said.\n\n\"Unpleasantly?\" Iroh asked.\n\n\"No. Just strange.\"\n\nHe ordered another.\n\nThat was an endorsement.\n\nAt sunset, we counted the sample cups. We had sold enough to justify a small batch, not enough to pretend the new blend would replace everything else. Iroh wrote the order on the proper sheet, including fair payment for the leaves and enough time for staff preparation.\n\nThe first jar sat beside the window.\n\nBoth our names were on it.\n\n\"What do we call it?\" I asked.\n\nHe looked at the rain sliding down the glass.\n\n\"Something simple.\"\n\n\"Not 'The General's Triumphant Citrus'?\"\n\n\"An excellent way to frighten customers.\"\n\n\"Then 'After the Rain'?\"\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"'Jasmine After Rain,'\" he said.\n\nI smiled.\n\nIt sounded like the kind of name a person might remember without having to know the history behind it.\n\nWe cleaned the kitchen together. Iroh washed the mortar while I dried the cups. When I tried to take the last heavy pot from him, he looked at me over the rim.\n\n\"I am capable of washing one pot.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why are you rescuing me?\"\n\nI laughed.\n\n\"Bad habit.\"\n\n\"Perhaps we could both practice letting the other finish.\"\n\nWe did.\n\nAt the end of the evening, with the kitchen finally quiet, he drew me close and asked if I wanted to stay. I kissed him before answering, then pulled back to give the question its proper words.\n\n\"Yes. Tonight I do.\"\n\nHe smiled against my cheek.\n\nWe turned out the stove, left the first jar labeled in two different hands, and went upstairs.\n\nIn the morning, the jar was still there.\n\nSo was the recipe, complete with cost, drying time, and the words Mina had added at the bottom:\n\n*Please do not set the kitchen on fire while inventing the next one.*","word_count":1671}},"love-and-duty":{"Love-And-Duty-09":{"number":9,"title":"Water Remembers","content":"Katara kissed me six days after Aang did.\n\nNot that I was counting.\n\nToph was counting.\n\nShe announced the number at breakfast.\n\n“Six.”\n\nAang looked up from his fruit. “Six what?”\n\n“Days.”\n\nKatara closed her eyes. “Toph.”\n\n“What? I like statistics now. Paperwork’s been a bad influence.”\n\nI threw a napkin at her.\n\nToph caught it and wiped her mouth.\n\nThe waiting had become its own kind of intimacy.\n\nKatara did not avoid me. If anything, she seemed more deliberate. She touched my hand when she passed me something. Sat close enough that our knees met under tables. Asked whether I wanted to walk home with her after late clinic shifts.\n\nShe never used those moments to corner me into the kiss everyone knew was coming.\n\nThat made me want it more.\n\nOn the sixth day, rain came down hard enough to flood the lower market.\n\nCranefish Town’s drainage system had improved from “actively hostile” to “merely inadequate,” which meant the southern channel overflowed into three streets and a warehouse courtyard.\n\nBy noon I was soaked to the skin.\n\nKatara stood in the middle of the street redirecting water toward the river in broad, controlled sweeps. She worked with two local waterbenders, but the volume was too much for finesse. Brown floodwater rolled around my calves.\n\n“Get the records upstairs!” I shouted to the warehouse staff.\n\nA worker slipped near the loading ramp.\n\nI caught their arm.\n\nThe current hit both of us.\n\nFor one horrible second my feet left the ground.\n\nThe world became water and noise.\n\nThen something wrapped around my waist and yanked me sideways.\n\nA ribbon of clean water.\n\nKatara.\n\nShe pulled me onto the steps hard enough that I collided with her.\n\nHer hands gripped my shoulders.\n\n“Are you hurt?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Are you sure?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHer face was furious.\n\nNot at me.\n\nAt the possibility.\n\nI touched her wrist. “Katara.”\n\nShe breathed once.\n\nTwice.\n\nThe anger changed shape.\n\n“Don’t do that again.”\n\n“I’ll schedule future floods more responsibly.”\n\nHer mouth twitched despite herself.\n\n“Go inside.”\n\n“I can help.”\n\n“You can help by not making me rescue you twice.”\n\n“Twice?”\n\n“You count the first day with the wall.”\n\n“That was not water.”\n\n“I’m counting it.”\n\n“Toph is ruining all of you.”\n\nKatara pushed wet hair away from her face. “Inside.”\n\nI obeyed.\n\nMostly because I knew the difference between care and control now, and because Katara had work to do that would be easier if she was not watching me drown.\n\nThe flood receded by evening.\n\nWe ended up at the clinic wrapped in borrowed blankets while our clothes dried near the stove. Aang had gone to inspect the upstream gate. Toph was helping stabilize a collapsed retaining wall.\n\nFor once, Katara and I were alone.\n\nShe sat across from me at the little kitchen table, hair loose around her shoulders.\n\nWithout her usual braid and arm wraps, she looked younger.\n\nNot less capable.\n\nSimply tired enough to let the armor loosen.\n\n“You scared me,” she said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“I hate that.”\n\n“Being scared?”\n\n“Not being able to control what happens to people I love.”\n\nThe word love slipped into the room so naturally that neither of us reacted at first.\n\nThen Katara’s eyes widened.\n\nI felt my breath stop.\n\nShe did not take it back.\n\nInstead she looked down at her cup.\n\n“I don’t mean…”\n\nMy stomach dropped.\n\nKatara frowned. “No. That’s not right. I do mean it.”\n\nI waited.\n\nShe drew a slow breath.\n\n“I love you,” she said. “I don’t know exactly what shape it is yet. I know it isn’t the same as what I feel for Aang because nothing is the same as anything else. But I love you.”\n\nThe honesty was terrifying.\n\nNot because I did not feel it.\n\nBecause I did.\n\n“I think I love you too.”\n\nKatara looked up.\n\n“Think?”\n\n“Don’t make me regret being careful with words.”\n\nHer smile arrived, warm and a little wicked.\n\n“I would never.”\n\n“You absolutely would.”\n\nShe stood.\n\nMy pulse accelerated.\n\nKatara walked around the table and stopped in front of me.\n\n“May I?”\n\nI knew exactly what she meant.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHer hand cupped the side of my face.\n\nKatara’s kiss was nothing like Aang’s.\n\nAang had kissed like joy discovering permission.\n\nKatara kissed like water finding the shape of a shoreline.\n\nSlow first.\n\nCertain.\n\nHer thumb rested below my ear. The other hand settled at my shoulder. She gave me time to meet her, time to pull away, time to decide how close I wanted to be.\n\nI rose from the chair.\n\nThat was my answer.\n\nThe kiss deepened.\n\nWarmth moved through me despite the damp chill still clinging to my skin. Katara’s breath caught when my hand found her waist. She leaned closer, and for a few seconds there was no flood, no city, no endless stack of reports waiting tomorrow.\n\nOnly the quiet sound of rain against the clinic windows.\n\nWhen we separated, our foreheads stayed together.\n\nKatara laughed softly.\n\n“What?” I whispered.\n\n“I was trying to be patient.”\n\n“You were.”\n\n“For six days.”\n\nI pulled back enough to stare at her.\n\n“You knew Toph was counting?”\n\n“Of course I knew.”\n\nI laughed.\n\nKatara kissed me again, shorter this time.\n\nThen the back door opened.\n\nToph walked in covered in mud.\n\nShe stopped.\n\nHer head tilted.\n\n“Finally.”\n\nKatara picked up a towel and threw it at her.\n\nToph caught it with one hand.\n\n“Seven minutes late,” she said.\n\n“What does that mean?” I asked.\n\n“I told Aang it would happen before sunset.”\n\n“You bet on us?”\n\n“No money. He’s a monk.”\n\n“I am not taking relationship criticism from someone who still hasn’t kissed anybody,” Katara said.\n\nToph’s expression vanished.\n\nSilence.\n\nI felt the air change.\n\nKatara immediately softened. “Toph, I didn’t mean—”\n\n“I know.”\n\nToph rubbed the towel over her hair.\n\nThe bravado came back, but I had heard the crack beneath it.\n\nI stood.\n\n“Do you want tea?” I asked.\n\nToph frowned. “That’s your response?”\n\n“To what?”\n\nHer mouth twitched.\n\n“You’re annoying.”\n\n“Tea?”\n\n“Strong.”\n\nKatara moved toward the kettle.\n\nAs she passed Toph, she touched the back of her hand.\n\nToph turned her palm up.\n\nTheir fingers caught for a heartbeat.\n\nSmall.\n\nUnannounced.\n\nBut real.\n\nWater remembered every shape it touched.\n\nMaybe love did too.\n\nNot by becoming the same shape.\n\nBy learning how to move around each person without erasing them.\n\nAang returned twenty minutes later soaked, cheerful, and carrying three bags of dumplings.\n\nToph immediately told him he had lost the bet.\n\nKatara denied there had been a bet.\n\nI refused to confirm anything.\n\nAang looked at my face, looked at Katara, and smiled.\n\nHe kissed Katara first.\n\nThen he squeezed my shoulder.\n\nNo jealousy.\n\nNo performance.\n\nJust recognition.\n\nThe four of us ate dumplings at the clinic table while our clothes dried and rain ran harmlessly through the newly cleared channels outside.\n\nMy mouth still remembered Katara.\n\nMy hand rested close to hers.\n\nEvery few minutes, our fingers touched.\n\nNeither of us pretended it was accidental.\n\nThe first time Katara let me help in the clinic after that, she gave me the least glamorous task available: washing bandages.\n\nI suspected this was intentional.\n\n“You’re smiling,” she said from the next basin.\n\n“I’m thrilled by linen.”\n\n“You’re terrible at sarcasm.”\n\n“I learned from Toph.”\n\n“That explains it.”\n\nWater curled from Katara’s fingertips, lifting soap from the cloth in clean ribbons. I had to scrub mine by hand. The difference should have been embarrassing. Instead it became companionable—the rhythm of work divided according to what each of us could do.\n\nAfter a while, Katara said, “You know I don’t need you to become useful enough to earn this.”\n\nMy hands stopped under the water.\n\n“This?”\n\n“Us.”\n\nI looked down at the bandage between my palms.\n\nShe had done it again: walked directly into a fear I had hidden from myself and then stood there without making a spectacle of finding it.\n\n“I know,” I said.\n\nKatara’s eyebrow rose.\n\n“I am trying to know.”\n\n“That’s different.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nShe bumped her shoulder against mine.\n\n“Good.”\n\nWe worked until the basket was empty.\n\nOne cloth remained caught beneath the wicker rim. Katara worked it loose with a fingernail and found an old clinic stamp half washed out on the corner.\n\n\"That one's done,\" she said.\n\n\"Because of the ink?\"\n\n\"Because it's fraying. The next patient shouldn't have to trust a bandage I already know will tear.\"\n\nShe folded it separately. I watched the precision of her hands and remembered how, a few hours earlier, she'd been less certain about touching mine.\n\n\"I want to ask about Aang,\" I said.\n\nKatara glanced up. \"That sounds ominous.\"\n\n\"It's not. I love him. I'm finding out what loving you means too. I'm ethically non-monogamous, and I prefer polyamory. I don't want either of you to hear that as a demand to feel the same things on the same schedule.\"\n\n\"I already love Aang,\" she said. \"That isn't what frightens me.\"\n\n\"What does?\"\n\n\"That people will start calling me generous for agreeing to something I chose. As if my feelings are a favor I'm doing for the Avatar.\"\n\nI made a face. \"They could just ask you.\"\n\n\"They could.\" Her mouth quirked. \"You may notice a theme in my day.\"\n\nShe handed me the frayed cloth for the repair pile. Our fingers caught around the edge; neither of us pretended not to notice. I could still taste the tea she'd made after the rain, and I wanted another kiss badly enough that I didn't trust myself to phrase it elegantly.\n\n\"I'd like to kiss you when we finish,\" I said.\n\n\"Good,\" she replied. \"I was beginning to think you only came here for laundry.\"\n\nWhen we carried the clean cloths inside, she did not take the heavier stack from me automatically. She asked.\n\nI said I had it.\n\nShe believed me.\n\nLater, when my arms tired, I asked her to take half.\n\nShe did that too.\n\nWater remembered the shape of every container without becoming trapped by any of them. Katara had once told me that was part of its strength.\n\nI was beginning to understand why she loved it.\n\nKatara squeezed my hand before letting go.\n\n“Learning to ask is part of strength too,” she said.\n\nI wanted to argue that she was stealing her own advice from me. Instead, I carried the lighter stack and let her carry the rest.\n\nFor once, neither of us made sacrifice into a competition.\n\nKatara smiled like she knew exactly how hard that tiny request had been. She did not praise me for it. Somehow that made it easier to do again.\n","word_count":1777},"Love-And-Duty-24":{"number":24,"title":"Aang’s Terrible, Wonderful Idea","content":"Aang’s terrible idea began with the sentence, “Technically, I checked the rules.”\n\nI put down my tea.\n\nKatara closed her eyes.\n\nToph grinned.\n\n“No,” Katara said.\n\n“You don’t know what it is.”\n\n“I know you.”\n\n“That’s unfair.”\n\n“Is it?”\n\nAang stood in our kitchen wearing the expression he had whenever being the Avatar temporarily became less important than being the same person who once invented an air scooter because walking was boring.\n\nHe held four paper tickets.\n\nToph tilted her head. “Race?”\n\nAang looked betrayed. “How did you know?”\n\n“You’re vibrating.”\n\n“I am not.”\n\n“You are emotionally vibrating.”\n\nI took one ticket from his hand.\n\nWINTER FOUNDERS’ FESTIVAL — CHARITY STREET COURSE.\n\nBelow it, in smaller print:\n\nNON-MOTORIZED VEHICLES ONLY. BENDING-PROPULSION PERMITTED IN OPEN CLASS.\n\nI looked at Aang.\n\n“No.”\n\nHis face fell.\n\n“I haven’t explained.”\n\n“You want us to enter.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“In what vehicle?”\n\nHis grin returned.\n\nThat was the second warning.\n\nThe vehicle was in the courtyard.\n\nCalling it a vehicle was generous.\n\nIt looked like a tea cart had fallen in love with a glider and produced offspring during a storm.\n\nFour wheels. Wooden frame. Cloth wing. Steering bar. Two seats in front, two in back. A metal plate ran beneath the chassis for Toph to bend against if necessary. A small water reservoir sat behind Katara’s seat.\n\nI stared.\n\nKatara stared.\n\nToph walked around it once, feeling the metal through her feet.\n\n“This is the stupidest thing you’ve ever built.”\n\nAang beamed. “Thank you.”\n\n“I wasn’t complimenting you.”\n\n“I’m keeping it.”\n\nI circled the machine.\n\n“Who designed the brakes?”\n\nAang pointed at Sokka’s signature scratched into the rear axle.\n\nI relaxed slightly.\n\nThen I noticed a second note underneath.\n\nTOPH CHANGED THEM. GOOD LUCK.\n\nI stopped relaxing.\n\nThe race was two days later.\n\nOn the first trial run, Aang insisted the machine only needed a little more lift.\n\n\"How much is a little?\" Katara asked.\n\n\"Enough to make it fun.\"\n\nToph stomped once beside the axle. \"The left brake is loose.\"\n\nAang looked down. \"I checked it.\"\n\n\"You checked whether it existed. Different question, Twinkle Toes.\"\n\nI wanted to laugh, but a delivery cart was coming across the testing yard. Aang's gust lifted our front wheel an inch before Katara seized the frame and demanded he stop. The cart driver had to pull back hard to avoid us.\n\nHis name was Beren, and he wasn't interested in whether the Avatar had meant well. He pointed to the rut his cart had left.\n\n\"That's where my load goes every morning. If your race blocks the route, someone else misses their delivery.\"\n\nAang's face fell. \"I didn't realize this was the only usable way through.\"\n\n\"Then ask before you decide it isn't.\"\n\nToph crouched, laid one hand against the axle and found the loose connection. She wouldn't certify the fix until Sokka checked the drawing. Katara walked the planned path with Beren to see where a temporary crossing could actually be made. I measured the width and wrote down what he said instead of guessing.\n\nAang helped move the testing frame out of the way.\n\nBeren wasn't finished. He set a crate of cabbages on the ground, opened the lid and showed us three split heads beneath the sound ones.\n\n\"That happened when I stopped,\" he said.\n\nAang looked stricken. \"I'll pay for them.\"\n\n\"That's easy for you to say. I need the market stall to take the rest before the street warms. Money later doesn't put my deliveries back on time.\"\n\nKatara asked which stall he used and whether it would accept the crates an hour late. I wrote the address on the back of our test plan. Toph stood with one boot against the axle, still watching the brake connection that had come loose.\n\n\"Your rig's not rolling again today,\" she said.\n\nAang started to protest, then saw the split cabbages. \"Okay. What do you need right now?\"\n\n\"Room to leave,\" Beren said.\n\nWe cleared the crossing. Aang helped carry the damaged crate to a safer cart, asking first because the load had been balanced for a particular route. Katara accompanied Beren through the market gate so the stall owner would understand the delay. There was no applause for the Avatar, and Beren didn't magically forgive him because he'd been useful for ten minutes.\n\nWhen Katara returned, she handed me a note: the stall had accepted the salvageable vegetables but deducted a small portion for bruising. We paid that loss from the festival testing budget, not from Beren's own wages.\n\nAang looked at the receipt and sighed.\n\n\"Next time,\" he said, \"I ask who uses the road before I invent a flying one.\"\n\nToph slapped the axle. \"And I check whether the thing can stop before you give it wings.\"\n\n\"You could still make it fly,\" I told him as we cleared the yard.\n\n\"I know.\" He looked toward Beren's wagon. \"But then we'd have to land somewhere.\"\n\n\"Congratulations,\" Toph said. \"You invented consequences.\"\n\nAang laughed. He sounded a little embarrassed, which was an improvement over his first plan to take the corner by air.\n\nWe spent those days arguing about roles.\n\nAang wanted to steer.\n\nKatara said Aang’s idea of steering included vertical space.\n\nToph wanted control of the brakes because they were metal.\n\nI wanted to live.\n\nWe compromised.\n\nPoorly.\n\nOn race morning, Cranefish Town was packed with spectators. The route wound from the market district, across the eastern bridge, around the old refinery, and down a long slope to the river square.\n\nCompetitors included messenger carts, sand-sailer adaptations, a pair of Fire Nation engineers with a pedal-driven machine, and one old woman pushing what appeared to be a wheelbarrow with absolute confidence.\n\nToph pointed toward her.\n\n“She’s the threat.”\n\n“You can tell?” I asked.\n\n“She has no fear.”\n\nAang climbed into the front seat.\n\nKatara sat beside him.\n\nToph and I took the rear.\n\n“This is a date,” Aang announced.\n\n“This is attempted murder,” Katara replied.\n\n“Romantic murder.”\n\n“Drive.”\n\nThe starting gong sounded.\n\nEverything became noise.\n\nAang launched us forward with a burst of air.\n\nI screamed.\n\nToph laughed.\n\nKatara shouted, “LEFT!”\n\nAang shouted, “I KNOW!”\n\nWe turned right.\n\nThe Fire Nation pedal machine clipped a stack of hay bales. The wheelbarrow grandmother passed us on the inside.\n\nToph slapped both feet against the floor plate and bent the rear axle just enough to pivot us around a vegetable cart.\n\nA cabbage rolled through the air.\n\nSomewhere, I felt history repeat itself.\n\n“We’re going too fast!” I yelled.\n\nAang looked over his shoulder.\n\n“We’re in seventh!”\n\n“That is not an answer!”\n\nKatara bent a ribbon of water from the reservoir and froze it along one runner as we hit a patch of mud. The cart slid sideways, perfectly controlled for approximately two seconds and then absolutely not controlled at all.\n\nToph whooped.\n\nI discovered new prayers.\n\nWe reached the eastern bridge in fourth place.\n\nAang could have won easily if he stopped pretending the machine mattered and simply airbent us down the route.\n\nHe refused.\n\n“That’s cheating!”\n\n“Bending propulsion is allowed!” Katara yelled.\n\n“Too much is spiritually cheating!”\n\nToph shouted, “Your spirituality is losing!”\n\nThe bridge narrowed.\n\nTwo carts entered ahead of us side by side.\n\nAang pulled back.\n\nI expected frustration.\n\nInstead he laughed.\n\n“We go over.”\n\nKatara’s eyes widened. “Aang—”\n\nAir hit beneath the cloth wing.\n\nThe cart lifted.\n\nNot high.\n\nHigh enough.\n\nWe sailed over the gap between the two competitors and landed with every wheel striking stone at once.\n\nMy teeth met hard enough to see stars.\n\nToph was laughing so violently she could barely bend.\n\nKatara stared at Aang.\n\nHe grinned.\n\nShe grabbed his face and kissed him while he was still steering.\n\n“We are going to die,” I said.\n\nToph slapped my knee. “Worth it!”\n\nThe refinery turn brought us beside the wheelbarrow grandmother.\n\nShe glanced over.\n\n“Too many people!” she shouted.\n\n“You’re terrifying!” Aang shouted back.\n\nShe smiled.\n\nThen passed us again.\n\nWe finished third.\n\nThe grandmother won.\n\nNobody was surprised.\n\nAt the award table, Aang bowed to her.\n\n“What’s your secret?”\n\nShe looked at him as if the Avatar were a somewhat slow child.\n\n“Downhill.”\n\nToph nearly collapsed.\n\nThe prize money went to the city’s winter shelter fund. Our third-place ribbon went to Toph, who claimed she had contributed the most by preventing the rest of us from dying.\n\nKatara argued that she had prevented at least two deaths personally.\n\nI argued that I had served as ballast.\n\nAang argued that joy was its own contribution.\n\nWe threw snow at him.\n\nThat should have been the end of the day.\n\nInstead, Aang dragged us to the festival proper.\n\nWe ate fried dough. Katara won a carved polar bear dog at a ring toss and insisted she had not used waterbending. Toph challenged a strength-test machine and broke it. I spent an irresponsible amount of money on roasted chestnuts.\n\nFor six hours, nobody asked Aang to mediate anything.\n\nNobody asked Katara to heal them.\n\nNobody asked Toph to build, teach, detect lies, or represent metalbenders.\n\nNobody handed me a form.\n\nWe were not symbols.\n\nWe were four idiots sharing one scarf because Aang had lost his, Toph refused to wear one properly, and Katara had decided hypothermia was a collective moral failure.\n\nNear sunset, music began in the square.\n\nAang reached for my hand.\n\nThis time he did not scan the crowd first.\n\nI noticed.\n\nHe noticed me noticing.\n\nHis fingers tightened.\n\n“Dance?”\n\n“I saw how you drove.”\n\n“That’s unrelated.”\n\n“It feels related.”\n\nKatara took my other hand.\n\n“Come on.”\n\nToph said, “I don’t dance.”\n\nThree of us looked at her.\n\nShe sighed.\n\n“I hate this family.”\n\n“You chose us,” Aang said.\n\n“Worst mistake of my life.”\n\nShe joined anyway.\n\nAang moved like air even with his boots on the ground.\n\nKatara followed rhythm the way she followed water, graceful until she laughed too hard and missed a step.\n\nToph danced by feeling everyone else’s feet through the stone and then deliberately doing something different.\n\nI was not good.\n\nNobody cared.\n\nAt one point Aang spun me beneath his arm, Katara caught me when I overbalanced, and Toph complained that our choreography was “aggressively sighted.”\n\nSo we changed it.\n\nToph put one hand on my shoulder, one on Katara’s. Aang kept contact through our linked arms. We stopped relying on visual cues and moved to pressure, rhythm, laughter, the vibration of drums under our feet.\n\nToph smiled.\n\nNot because we had included her.\n\nBecause we had adapted together without turning adaptation into charity.\n\nThat distinction mattered.\n\nNight settled over the square.\n\nLanterns reflected in patches of melted snow.\n\nAang pressed his forehead to mine when the music slowed.\n\n“Terrible idea?”\n\nI looked at Katara, cheeks pink from cold and dancing. Toph had stolen the third-place ribbon and tied it around her wrist like a championship belt.\n\n“Terrible,” I said.\n\nAang smiled.\n\n“Wonderful?”\n\nI kissed him.\n\n“Unfortunately.”\n\nThe terrible idea was an Air Nomad cultural center with a public kitchen, meditation hall, archive room, rooftop garden, and absolutely no clear source of funding.\n\nAang had drawn the first plan himself.\n\nThis was obvious because every corridor curved beautifully and none of the storage rooms were large enough to hold anything larger than a monk’s optimism.\n\nKatara spread the papers over the table. “Where do supplies go?”\n\nAang pointed. “There.”\n\n“That’s a window.”\n\n“It could become shelves.”\n\nToph leaned over the plan, unable to see it and therefore judging it with complete confidence. “Floor’s bad.”\n\n“You haven’t touched the floor. It doesn’t exist yet.”\n\n“I can tell.”\n\nI traced the public entrance. “If this is for teaching Air Nomad culture, who gets to teach?”\n\nAang’s excitement faltered.\n\nThat was the real question beneath all the architecture.\n\n“I do, at first,” he said.\n\n“And when people know parts you don’t?” Katara asked gently.\n\nHe looked at her.\n\nThere were scraps of Air Nomad history scattered across the world: temple records, old songs remembered imperfectly by families, artifacts in private collections, stories altered by a century of retelling. Aang carried more than anyone alive. He did not carry everything.\n\n“I invite them,” he said slowly.\n\nToph tapped the table. “Even if they disagree with you?”\n\nHis mouth twisted.\n\n“Especially then, probably.”\n\n“That sounds awful.”\n\n“It does.”\n\nI smiled. “Still wonderful.”\n\nAang looked at the plans again, this time with less certainty and more possibility.\n\nBy midnight we had added storage, ramps, a community advisory table, a rule against treating every surviving artifact as sacred merely because it was old, and an entire page labeled THINGS AANG DOES NOT HAVE TO KNOW ALONE.\n\nHe circled that heading.\n\nThen he drew a tiny sky bison beside it.\n\nToph felt the fresh ink with one fingertip.\n\n“Terrible drawing,” she said.\n\n“You can’t see it.”\n\n“I can feel the lack of talent.”\n\nWonderful, unfortunately, survived another meeting.\n","word_count":2143},"Love-And-Duty-39":{"number":39,"title":"Love and Duty","content":"The message arrived during our first real dinner in the rebuilt house.\n\nOf course it did.\n\nA young Air Acolyte stood at the courtyard gate soaked in rain, holding a sealed dispatch from the western district.\n\nA spirit had appeared near a rail excavation.\n\nWorkers were missing.\n\nThe local magistrate requested the Avatar immediately.\n\nAang read the message once.\n\nThen again.\n\nNobody spoke.\n\nThe old pattern stood up in the room with us.\n\nAang leaves.\n\nWe understand.\n\nThe world needs him.\n\nDinner cools.\n\nLove becomes the quiet thing waiting after duty finishes.\n\nAang folded the dispatch.\n\n“How long?” Katara asked.\n\nThe Acolyte shook his head. “We don’t know. The rail camp is two hours by bison.”\n\nAang looked toward the table.\n\nFour bowls.\n\nSteam still rising.\n\nToph’s foot pressed into the floor.\n\nI felt anger begin before I wanted it.\n\nNot at the missing workers.\n\nAt timing. At history. At the unfairness of loving someone whose responsibility could walk through the gate at any moment wearing wet clothes.\n\nAang looked at us.\n\n“I have to go.”\n\n“Yes,” Katara said.\n\nToph’s jaw tightened.\n\n“Yes,” I said.\n\nAang closed his eyes for half a second.\n\nThen he turned to the Acolyte.\n\n“Go tell the magistrate I’m coming. Ask the school to prepare Appa, emergency supplies, and two spirit-response teams.”\n\nThe Acolyte bowed and ran.\n\nAang did not move.\n\nToph tilted her head.\n\n“Well?”\n\n“I have ten minutes.”\n\nThe sentence changed everything.\n\nNot much.\n\nTen minutes did not solve the conflict.\n\nBut old Aang would already have been gone.\n\nKatara’s expression softened.\n\n“Eat.”\n\nHe sat.\n\nWe ate quickly.\n\nNot pretending the message had not arrived.\n\nAang explained what he knew. I pulled a regional map from the office. Toph identified the excavation geology from survey records she had reviewed months earlier. Katara packed medical supplies for him to take.\n\nHome did not compete with duty.\n\nIt prepared him for it.\n\nAt the gate, Aang kissed Katara.\n\nToph.\n\nMe.\n\nThen he stopped.\n\n“Come with me?”\n\nAll three of us stared.\n\nAang laughed nervously. “Not because I’m volunteering you. I’m asking.”\n\nToph’s mouth twitched.\n\n“What do you need?” Katara asked.\n\n“Toph knows the ground. You know field medicine. Paperwork knows the rail contracts and worker camps.”\n\nHe looked at each of us.\n\n“But no is allowed.”\n\nThere it was.\n\nThe repair turned into practice.\n\nKatara said yes.\n\nToph said, “Obviously.”\n\nI said yes after checking the council emergency rota and assigning my morning work to someone else.\n\nProgress looked ridiculous sometimes.\n\nWe left together.\n\nThe rail camp had cut too close to an ancient hot spring sacred to a local spirit.\n\nNot maliciously.\n\nIgnorantly.\n\nSurvey maps marked the spring as geothermal runoff. Workers had driven steel pilings through the stone chamber beneath it.\n\nThe spirit responded by filling the excavation with dense white fog and leading anyone who entered in circles until they collapsed from exhaustion.\n\nFourteen workers were missing.\n\nAang did not attack the spirit.\n\nOf course he did not.\n\nHe sat at the edge of the fog and listened.\n\nThat looked like doing nothing to several terrified supervisors.\n\nToph nearly punched one when he said so.\n\nKatara set up a treatment station.\n\nI pulled contracts.\n\nThe rail company had been warned about the spring by three local families and ignored them because the objections had not been filed through the central survey office.\n\nPaperwork again.\n\nA mechanism for accountability.\n\nA weapon when used to dismiss knowledge that arrived in the wrong format.\n\nI felt sick.\n\nToph placed both hands on the ground.\n\n“The chamber’s cracking,” she said. “If they pull the pilings wrong, it collapses.”\n\nAang opened his eyes.\n\n“The spirit is protecting it.”\n\nA supervisor snapped, “The spirit trapped fourteen people!”\n\nAang stood.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHis voice was calm.\n\n“And your company drove steel through its home after local residents told you not to.”\n\nThe man’s mouth closed.\n\nAang looked toward the fog.\n\n“Both things can be wrong.”\n\nThat became our task.\n\nNot choosing one innocent side.\n\nGetting people out while repairing what people had damaged.\n\nToph mapped the underground chamber through vibration. Aang entered the fog with a line tied around his waist—not because he needed restraint, but because the others did. Katara moved along the perimeter, using water from the spring to mark safe paths where the spirit allowed passage.\n\nI stood beside local residents and wrote down the names the company had ignored.\n\nOne woman, old enough that her hands shook, told me the spring had been protected for six generations.\n\n“We said it.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“No. You know now.”\n\nThe words struck like Toph.\n\nI wrote them exactly.\n\nAll fourteen workers came out alive.\n\nThe last one emerged leaning on Aang just before dawn.\n\nThe spirit appeared then.\n\nNot fully human. Not animal. A shape of steam, antlers, water, and light rising over the shattered spring.\n\nEveryone backed away except Aang.\n\nToph remained beside him because of course she did.\n\nKatara stood on his other side.\n\nI stayed where I was.\n\nSome moments belonged to people who could speak the world’s older languages.\n\nAang bowed.\n\nHe promised the pilings would be removed.\n\nThe company supervisor started to object.\n\nI handed him the contract clause allowing emergency rerouting where construction endangered protected cultural sites.\n\nHe stared.\n\nI smiled.\n\nPaperwork could have a dramatic entrance occasionally.\n\nThe spirit dissolved into rain.\n\nThe supervisor tried to leave before the workers could give their statements. One of the women whose family had warned about the spring stepped directly into his path.\n\n\"Your forms told us we weren't qualified to know where the water went,\" she said. \"Now you want our names on a report saying you fixed it?\"\n\n\"We have to notify the central office.\"\n\n\"Then notify them we warned you.\"\n\nI had a ledger and a box of damp pencils. I asked the workers to tell me what they had seen, not what the company had instructed them to say. Several were frightened about losing shifts if they contradicted the foreman. The woman from the spring offered her front room as a place for them to speak together, with a witness of their choosing.\n\nAang had just bowed to a spirit. He was exhausted and streaked with mud. He could have given a beautiful speech that settled nothing. Instead, he turned to the supervisor.\n\n\"Work stays stopped until the spring and pilings are independently assessed,\" he said. \"The workers' wages don't stop because the company made an unsafe choice.\"\n\nKatara looked up from binding a miner's wrist. \"And the injury reports go to the healers, not only the people who caused the injuries.\"\n\nToph tested the ground beyond the rail tie. \"Nobody moves that beam until somebody's checked the chamber underneath it. And if somebody says the ground's fine because they're wearing a fancy hat, send them to me.\"\n\nThe supervisor didn't look pleased. The workers did not cheer. They started writing their own names on a page that would be copied to more than one office, because one forgotten complaint was how this had begun.\n\nI stood back to let the woman from the spring read the final line before we sent it. She corrected the name of the old path. We changed it. It mattered that the document sounded like the people who had lived there.\n\nBy sunrise, the fog thinned.\n\nWe sat on a rail tie eating cold buns from the emergency pack.\n\nAang looked exhausted.\n\nToph leaned against his shoulder.\n\nKatara’s hair had escaped every tie.\n\nI had mud inside one boot.\n\n“This was our first dinner,” I said.\n\nAang winced.\n\n“Technically we ate it.”\n\n“Fast.”\n\n“Together.”\n\nToph pointed a bun at him. “Don’t get sentimental. I haven’t slept.”\n\nKatara rested her head against my shoulder.\n\n“Love and duty,” she said.\n\nAang looked toward the spring.\n\n“I keep acting like they’re opposite directions.”\n\n“Sometimes they are,” I said.\n\nHe turned toward me.\n\n“If duty always wins,” I continued, “love becomes the thing that pays for it.”\n\nAang was quiet.\n\n“And if love always wins,” Katara added, “you stop being who you are.”\n\nToph chewed her bun.\n\n“So stop making everything a contest.”\n\nWe looked at her.\n\nShe shrugged.\n\n“Sometimes you leave. Sometimes we come. Sometimes we say no. Sometimes the world waits ten minutes while you finish dinner.”\n\nAang smiled.\n\n“That’s annoyingly wise.”\n\n“I’m surrounded by idiots. Somebody has to do it.”\n\nThe sun rose over the rail camp.\n\nWorkers began dismantling the first section of track.\n\nLocal families stood with engineers, marking a new route.\n\nNot harmony.\n\nWork.\n\nI leaned against the people I loved.\n\nDuty was not proof that love mattered less.\n\nLove was not an exemption from duty.\n\nThe question was whether either became an excuse to stop choosing.\n\nThat morning, tired and muddy beside a wounded spring, we chose again.\n\nWe returned from the spring three days later smelling like wet stone and medicinal herbs.\n\nThe first thing Toph said when she opened the door was, “You both stink.”\n\nThe second was, “You’re late.”\n\nThe third never became words.\n\nShe caught Aang by the front of his robe and pulled him into a hug hard enough to make him wheeze.\n\nKatara embraced me at the same time, one hand settling at the back of my neck.\n\nNo one pretended we had not been afraid.\n\nDinner that night was leftovers reheated badly because none of us wanted to spend another hour apart in different rooms.\n\nAang told us about the injured spirit spring between bites. Katara corrected his medical details. I explained the negotiations with the nearby village. Toph interrupted to demand why neither of us had sent a messenger sooner.\n\n“We were busy.”\n\n“Bad answer.”\n\n“It’s the true one.”\n\n“Still bad.”\n\nAang reached across the table. “You’re right.”\n\nToph went quiet.\n\nThat was one of the hardest lessons duty kept teaching us: being needed elsewhere did not erase the effect of disappearing here.\n\nWe could have valid reasons and still owe one another repair.\n\n“I’m sorry,” I said. “Next time, information becomes part of the emergency plan.”\n\nKatara nodded. “Yes.”\n\nToph stabbed a vegetable with unnecessary force. “Good.”\n\nAang looked around the table.\n\n“Love and duty,” he said.\n\n“Don’t make it a slogan,” Toph warned.\n\n“It could be a very nice banner.”\n\n“I will burn it.”\n\n“You’re not a firebender.”\n\n“I know people.”\n\nWe laughed.\n\nThe choice renewed itself in the argument.\n\nThe next time duty called, we did better. Not perfectly. A messenger went out before we left. Katara wrote the destination. Aang added the expected return. I wrote what we knew and what we did not.\n\nToph later complained that our handwriting was terrible.\n\nShe also kept the message pinned beside the door until we came home.\n\nLove had not made duty smaller. It had made communication part of doing duty well.\n\nWe added the messenger rule to our household agreement that night. Toph complained about bureaucracy in romance while insisting the wording be precise.\n\nThat contradiction felt exactly like us.\n","word_count":1830}}};for(const s of lib.series){const rec=changes[s.series_slug]||{};for(const ch of s.chapters){const e=rec[ch.id];if(e&&ch.chapter_number===e.number){ch.title=e.title;ch.content=e.content;ch.word_count=e.word_count;}}}})();
